Give the sentient domain its drake surfaces and re-canonicalize the five tuple derivations

Dev accepted, in these words: "Accepted, build it" — the recommendation being that the eleven sentient
definitions — Sentient, Course, Direction, Distance, Effect, Ego, Gaze, Lean, Path, Percept, Waypoint, the
one example domain with no drake surface — take the surfaces `DrakeCLI emit` renders, that the five whose
derivation argument is a tuple (Direction, Ego, Lean, Percept, Waypoint) be re-canonicalized by parsing the
surface back, and that the eleven companions be replaced by the generator's output. No engine change, no
new test.

Why. After the example body sweep of ded1b5b every definition in the corpus was in the compact form except
the sentient domain, whose eleven definitions were JSON-only: authored in 2026-06 before drake existed as a
surface, never emitted, never parsed back. Five of them failed `parse(emit(td)) == td` for one reason: a
tuple argument to the parent — `Primal((Gaze, Distance))`, `Primal((Double, Double))`, `Primal((Percept,
Effect))` — stored in the derivation's type parameter as the pre-tree STRING `"(Gaze, Distance)"`, the form
before the parser built type-form trees on 2026-09-17. Every other pre-tree value type in the corpus was
re-canonicalized by a sweep that parsed its surface; the sentient domain had no surface for a sweep to
parse, so nothing ever touched these five leaves. The trio rule (DRACO.md §1) is that a type is three
artifacts that agree; the sentient types were two.

MEASURED before recommending, on a scratch copy with the compiled CLIs: `DrakeCLI emit --write` renders
eleven surfaces of one or two lines each — `type Waypoint from Sentient draco Primal((Gaze, Distance))`
and `domain domains sentient Sentient`; Sentient's `type Sentient from domains world World`, its domain
line and a `types [ … ]` list of its ten members. Parsing each surface back reproduces the committed JSON
byte for byte in six (Course, Distance, Effect, Gaze, Path, Sentient) and moves exactly ONE leaf in the
other five, `.dracoAspect.derivation[1].typeParameters[0]`, from the string to the Objective tree
`{"(,)": ["Gaze", "Distance"]}`; nothing else in any file. The Scala generated from either JSON is
whitespace-identical to the committed companions, which differ from the generator's output only by a
leading blank line (and one blank line more in Distance and Sentient; Sentient's trait line carried a
trailing space) — so the companions were already the generator's output in substance, and the example
gate's whitespace normalization is why it never saw the difference. `DrakeCLI check` on the finished copy:
surface drift 0, json drift 0. The compact form changes none of the eleven: every reference is own-package
or a derivation anchor, so no `uses` record arises.

Built as probed. The eleven `.drake` files are the emitter's output, copied in unchanged; the eleven JSON
files are `DrakeCLI parse` of those surfaces over the compiled classes — six byte-identical to the
committed files, five moved on the one leaf each; the eleven `.scala` files are `GeneratorCLI generate`
over the current resources, replacing the committed companions on whitespace alone. `DrakeCLI check
src/mods/resources/domains/sentient`: surface drift 0, json drift 0, skipped 0. No other file in the tree
changes but the four documents carrying ded1b5b's hash and this increment's records.

