THE DIRECT RUN FOUND A DEFECT THE PROBE HAD NOT. Every discovered suite on compiled classes with current main,
mods and test resources on the loader roots: 1005 tests, 78 suites including discovery, 1004 passed, ONE failed —
ExampleLoadingTest's "explicit two-stage loading resolves all 48 example definitions independently of declaration
order": `Ambiguous type 'Distance': domains.sentient.Distance, draco.base.Distance`, raised by `NameResolution.lookup`
from the type-form walk of Percept's and Waypoint's derivation argument `(Gaze, Distance)`. The 22 newly active
DrakeParseTest checks pass; every headline is as stated (surface losses 0 across 158 types, parse scope 110 + 48,
example gate 48 / 0, GenDrake 108 of 108, aspects 110, PON 80 / 550 / 42 and 7, scenario 23 clean, forest 1 of 2,
CO-DECLARATION categorical, pending mods actors 0). The cause: the old STRING leaf `"(Gaze, Distance)"` was host-opaque
text the resolver preserves without reading, so the ambiguity had been in the corpus since the sentient domain was
authored and never visible; the tree makes `Distance` a name, and a bare name resolves by UNIQUE ontology lookup
(Dev, 2026-10-20: local bindings, then unique lookup — no own-package precedence), which `draco.base.Distance` denies
it. The probe that grounded the recommendation ran the emitter, the parser, `DrakeCLI check` and the generator over
the compiled classes but not the two-stage LOADER, the one consumer that resolves type-form leaves — the gap in the
measurement, recorded here. MEASURED after the failure: (1) the whole ontology, 182 definitions canonical and
example as authored, holds exactly TWO ambiguous bare anchor parameters, Percept's and Waypoint's `Distance`; the
other duplicated simple names (ConsumeReport, Consumer, Creator, OriginateReport across the media; Generator; Value)
occur in no anchor parameter. (2) Spelled `domains.sentient.Distance` in full in the two headers — the convention the
2026-10-21 sweep set for a simple name that is not unique across the ontology, Decoder and Encoder in the Dracon
domain being spelled in full in their own package for the same reason — the resolver reads both, the emitter renders
`type Waypoint from Sentient draco Primal((Gaze, domains.sentient.Distance))`, `parse(emit)` reproduces the JSON byte
for byte, `DrakeCLI check` is clean, and the two companions regenerate with ONE line changed, `Primal[(Gaze,
domains.sentient.Distance)]` for `Primal[(Gaze, Distance)]` — valid Scala in the own package, no import. (3) The
COMPACT form of either spelling still fails: `NameResolution.rewrite` renders an anchor's type parameter bare in the
definition's own package unconditionally (`anchorParameter`, 2026-10-21), where the sweep's `short` renders a body leaf
bare only when the bare name is unique; `resolve` therefore writes `Distance` bare into the resolved definition and a
second read of it is ambiguous. No test compacts the example definitions and the two-stage loader does not re-read
its result, so the increment's gate does not meet it; it is an engine inconsistency on a collision the corpus had
never presented, the next related point. The build stopped there, the tree holding the eleven surfaces, the five
re-canonicalized JSON and the eleven companions as accepted, for Dev's decision on the correction.

